# main-service Environment Variables Example
PORT=5001
NODE_ENV=development
SERVICE_NAME=main-service
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
# Comma-separated allow-list. Also this service's CSRF defense (no separate
# CSRF token — see docs/security.md), so '*' is rejected outright in
# production (NODE_ENV=production): list explicit origins there.

# ── Auth verification (public key only — main-service never signs tokens) ──
# Must be byte-for-byte the SAME key/issuer/audience as auth-service's
# signing config (services/auth_services/.env) — this service verifies every
# request's JWT LOCALLY (see src/middleware/auth.js), with no network call
# back to auth-service. A mismatch here doesn't error loudly; it just makes
# every token look like INVALID_TOKEN after it's issued.
JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_BASE64=your_jwt_public_key_base64_encoded
JWT_ISSUER=hackathon-auth-service
JWT_AUDIENCE=patchline
# Optional: during a key rotation on auth-service, copy the OLD public key
# here (see services/auth_services/.env.example for the full rotation
# procedure). Lets this service keep accepting tokens signed under the old
# key until they naturally expire, instead of erroring on all of them the
# instant auth-service switches keys.
# JWT_PREVIOUS_PUBLIC_KEY_BASE64=

# Databases
# REDIS_URL: main-service's OWN Redis — BullMQ scan/fix queues, HTTP cache
# middleware, rate limiter, OAuth state. This is the higher-throughput
# instance; size/scale it independently from the one below.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# SHARED_REDIS_URL: the Redis instance auth-service and ai-storage-service
# actually run against. main-service only reads one key namespace from it —
# scan:stage:{scanId}, the real-time pipeline checkpoint ai-storage-service
# writes (see src/config/sharedRedis.js + docker-compose.yml's "Redis
# topology" comment). Optional: if unset, falls back to REDIS_URL, so a
# single-Redis deployment works exactly as before this split existed.
SHARED_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-supabase-project.supabase.co
# Service-role key only (bypasses RLS — server-side use only, never expose
# this to the frontend). main-service does not use the anon key.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your_supabase_service_role_key

# Shared secret attached to every call this service forwards to
# ai-storage-service (x-internal-service-token header) — must match
# INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN in services/ai_services/.env exactly.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN=change_me_internal_token

# Encrypts stored GitHub/Jira OAuth tokens at rest (see src/utils/crypto.js).
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
OAUTH_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY_BASE64=your_32_byte_key_base64_encoded

# ── Integrations: GitHub OAuth App (per-user connect, not app-wide) ──
# Register at https://github.com/settings/developers -> "New OAuth App".
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=your_github_client_id
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=your_github_client_secret
GITHUB_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5001/api/github/oauth/callback
GITHUB_SCOPES=read:user user:email repo

# ── Integrations: Jira OAuth 2.0 / 3LO (per-user connect) ──
# Register at https://developer.atlassian.com/console/myapps/. The callback
# URL registered there must exactly match JIRA_REDIRECT_URI below.
JIRA_CLIENT_ID=your_jira_client_id
JIRA_CLIENT_SECRET=your_jira_client_secret
JIRA_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5001/api/jira/oauth/callback
JIRA_SCOPES=read:jira-work write:jira-work read:jira-user offline_access
JIRA_PROJECT_KEY=HACK
JIRA_ISSUE_TYPE=Task

# ── Microservice endpoints ──
AI_STORAGE_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:5002
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Scanner background job queue (Redis + BullMQ) ──
# Scan/fix requests are enqueued and answered with 202 Accepted; workers do
# the actual work asynchronously (see src/workers/scannerWorkers.js). Uses
# REDIS_URL above. By default the workers run in-process with the API — set
# DISABLE_INLINE_WORKERS=true and run `node src/worker.js` (or `npm run
# worker`) as a separate process/container once you want scanner workers to
# scale independently from the API — see docs/deployment.md §
# "Scanner workers as their own scalable service" (also demonstrated in
# docker-compose.yml's `worker` service, which runs alongside these inline
# workers rather than requiring DISABLE_INLINE_WORKERS first).
#
# Real throughput = (worker replicas) × (this concurrency value), not just
# this value alone — see docs/deployment.md for why more replicas at a
# modest concurrency each scales further than one process at a high
# concurrency. Each concurrent scan job also fans out into its own bounded
# AI-call concurrency inside ai-storage-service (_AI_BATCH_CONCURRENCY,
# currently 4-6) — so SCAN_WORKER_CONCURRENCY × replicas × ~6 is roughly
# your peak concurrent AI-provider call count. Size this against your AI
# provider's actual rate limit, not just "higher is better."
DISABLE_INLINE_WORKERS=false
SCAN_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=5
FIX_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4
